 # BRDF and Relighting Extensions
 
-Also called: Relightable 3D Gaussian Splatting, Inverse Rendering with Gaussians, PBR 3DGS
+Also called: Relightable 3D Gaussian Splatting, PBR Gaussian Splatting
 
 ## Overview
-This family extends standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by decoupling material properties from lighting, enabling physically based rendering (PBR) and dynamic relighting [S1]. Unlike standard 3DGS, which bakes lighting into view-dependent spherical harmonics via an emission-absorption model, these methods store explicit BRDF parameters (albedo, roughness, normals) per Gaussian and evaluate shading models using environment maps or indirect lighting coefficients [S1]. This allows for the reconstruction of interreflections and the application of new lighting conditions without retraining the geometry [S1].
+This family extends standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by replacing baked color attributes with physically based rendering (PBR) parameters, enabling dynamic relighting, shadow casting, and accurate reflection modeling [S1]. Unlike standard 3DGS, which captures lighting in spherical harmonics via an emission-absorption model, these methods decouple geometry from material properties, allowing Gaussians to respond to arbitrary light sources and environment maps [S1]. They distinguish themselves from siblings like [3DGS-DR](1471-3dgs-dr.md) by explicitly storing BRDF parameters (albedo, roughness, normal) per Gaussian, facilitating direct lighting reconstruction via environment maps and indirect lighting via spherical harmonics or pre-filtered cubemaps [S1].
 
